A||||11||William Federle||Missing Iron Duck||||11||
Z||000000||William Federle||01-03-2000||10:07 PM||||Help - on my way home from work tonite in the freezing rain and snow I let off of the gas to slow down for a 55 zone and the car began to miss. It was interrmittant. It would run ok for a few seconds and then miss.When it missed, the shift light would go on and the tach would jump. When I got off at my exit and began driving at normal in town speeds (35 - 45) everything smoothed out. The only thing loose that I could find was the nuts to the air cleaner cover - I tightened them down finger tight, but I have a feeling that I haven't found the answer yet. This car is a 1984 Fiero SE 4 speed manual. Does anyone have any ideas? Thanks in advance.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-03-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000001||Eddie.H||01-03-2000||10:32 PM||||I call it hot @ cold when the motor warms up it runs lean when the thermo stat opens the cold water runs in to hot motor and the ecm trys to ajust for it by making the car run rich.  try changing the thermo stat to 185 you should have 195 in it now. 185 well let you have some heat and help keep the water moving.this will keep the motor going rich to lean to rich to lean.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||01-04-2000||03:21 AM||||You might want to check the terminal on the starter motor solonoid that the battery positive cable hooks to. (The big one)  The 84 models attatch 2 fusible links to this terminal that supply power to the rest of the vehicle.  Heat from the exhaust system tends to fry these links over a period of time and can cause strange symptoms like the ones you mentioned.  I've also had some trouble with the nut that secures the wires on that terminal coming loose and causing the same type problems. I installed a self locking nut and seem to have solved it.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Standard||01-04-2000||05:03 AM||||I'll vouch for that!  Both of mine were fried good back there!  Had to replace em both...||||reg||11
Z||000004||William Federle||01-04-2000||10:03 AM||||Thanks for the replies. I'm going to check on that as soon as I get the car in my garage tonight. On the thermostat, I can see how that might cause what you describe, but I can't see how it would cause the shift light to come on simultaneously with the other symptoms. However I think I might try the 180 deg thermostat - I read in a posting that using it would allow the coolant to flow full time and then the fan would be the main controller of engine heat. Thanks again everyone for your thoughts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||01-04-2000||10:28 AM||||My 84 4-sp did that too, and only when it rained.  Tach filter, maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||01-04-2000||03:00 PM||||I would suspect something getting wet. My 84 had a serious problem with heavy rain. If it was parked during a heavy rain, it would start fine but quit running after a couple of minutes. Then it WOULD NOT start until it sat for a couple of hours. It was like something had to dry out but I NEVER could figure out what. The grill directly over the engine on the 84s never did look a good wet weather design to me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-04-2000||11:01 PM||||At first I was thinking tach filter also. But, I don't think the 4-cyl had one. I could be wrong though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||01-04-2000||11:23 PM||||Well, I never got to try the 180 deg. thermostat. I stalled the car on my driveway while putting it into the garage and had one heck of a time restarting it. I jacked up the rear end and checked the big red wire on the starter solenoid - sure enough it was loose. It wouldn't tighten up though - the stud was cross-threaded. I got a lock nut and it tightened up - but before I got it tight on the wire terminal the stud came out of the solenoid. So now I guess I'll get to explore the wonderful world of starters. Anyway - it sort of answers a lot of questions. It's been hard to start and then the symptoms for which I started this thread appeared.||||reg||9
Z||000009||William Federle||01-17-2000||10:05 PM||||I got the new starter and solenoid installed and thought that my wet weather engine miss<BR>problems were solved. But today it was snowing in the afternoon, and when I started home my car began missing before I had driven the mile to the expressway. It misses and the tach jumps around and the shift light blinks on and off. The symptoms disappeared when the engine got up to operating temperature. They re-appeared when I got off of the expressway until I had driven about 1 mile and then went away again. I jacked up the car and looked at the wires on the starter solenoid - they were tight but I put a little more on the nut and then installed a jam-nut. I looked at the connectors on the coil and distributer - they had a real thick cruddy grease in them - I cleaned this out as best I could and put in dialectric grease. <P>Does any of this sound like I might be on the right track to anyone? I'd appreciate any comments or ideas.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||01-17-2000||10:35 PM||||56, not sure about other years, but my 86 4 banger has a tach filter. Needs replacement though I think since when I get up around 3500 it jumps way past the 6 grand mark and tries to push my oil pressure gauge down to 0 so it can try and make another revolution. At any rate, I'd at least look at getting it tested to see if that is the cause for your miss.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||01-17-2000||10:47 PM||||wet weather skip is almost guaranteed to be an ignition problem. replace the plugwires, dist cap, and rotor. make sure the coil is clean. make sure the dist module is clean. dirt draws water.||||reg||1
